WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.533
Milvus.

2
00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:04.919
The heavy scale option, built for billions of vectors.

3
00:00:04.919 --> 00:00:07.033
Let's see when that actually matters.

4
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:11.967
Milvus is built for billions of vectors under constant writes.

5
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:14.900
Its trick is to separate storage from compute.

6
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.567
Here is the architecture.

7
00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:20.800
At the bottom, object storage holds the segments.

8
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.500
Index building runs on its own nodes, away from queries.

9
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.100
And stateless query nodes on top scale out horizontally.

10
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.000
Here is the interface, locally.

11
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.433
Milvus lite is an embedded build with the same API as a fifty node cluster.

12
00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:44.367
Quick setup already built a default index, so we release the collection, drop it, and choose our own: one thousand twenty four cells.

13
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:47.049
We build it and load the collection.

14
00:00:47.049 --> 00:00:50.491
At query time, nprobe sets how many cells to search.

15
00:00:50.491 --> 00:00:53.733
Low is fast and lossy, high is slow and thorough.

16
00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:57.233
Six stores later, here is the whole map.

17
00:00:57.533 --> 00:01:01.533
FAISS is just a library, perfect for experiments on one machine.

18
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:05.667
Qdrant is the self-hosted production server, with rich filters.

19
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:09.667
Pinecone is managed, for when operations cost more than the bill.

20
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:11.632
And Milvus is for scale.

21
00:01:11.632 --> 00:01:13.367
Overkill, until it isn't.

22
00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:17.033
Picture the roadmap for Mission Control RAG.

23
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:24.200
The plan is to index every NASA technical report plus arXiv: about two billion chunks, growing daily.

24
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.933
That is where one Qdrant box stops being the answer, and separated storage becomes the point.

25
00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:37.800
Until then, milvus lite teaches you the API, and the map is your interview answer: it depends, and here is the axis.

26
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:39.752
Let's recap.

27
00:01:39.752 --> 00:01:42.133
Milvus separates storage from compute.

28
00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:44.467
It is built for billions of vectors.

29
00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:47.933
Milvus lite lets you learn the API on your laptop.

30
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:51.519
And you pick a store by scale and operations.

31
00:01:51.519 --> 00:01:52.833
Next up: GraphRAG.
